Primary driver for EDIT 1: Liu F, Liu Z, Fei X, He J, Xing J, Li J, Chan P. "Development of a
Framework for Evaluating Large Language Model Safety and Reliability: a Proof-of-Concept
Evaluation." J Med Syst 2026;50(1). doi 10.1007/s10916-026-02459-1, PMID 42776332.
Peer-reviewed; verified against PubMed metadata 24 Sep 2026. Department of Emergency, Xuanwu
Hospital / Capital Medical University, Beijing.

Design: 54 diagnostically challenging EMERGENCY cases from non-public institutional records. Six
LLMs via API (text-only, zero-shot, defaults; Aug-Dec 2025), each queried three times, yielding
972 PHYSICIAN-SCORED responses. Pre-specified error taxonomy, difficulty-stratified analysis,
two-layer response consistency.

Findings that carry:
  - **31 of 34 dangerous model-case combinations were STOCHASTIC, not systematic** — the dangerous
    recommendation appeared on some runs of a case and not others. The authors name this "a failure
    mode hidden by aggregate metrics."
  - Aggregate scores concealed heterogeneity: anchoring-bias failure varied nearly four-fold
    (6.9%-26.4%); rare-disease recognition six-fold (6.1%-37.9%).
  - Catastrophic (dangerous-recommendation) rates were two-tiered: 1.5% for the safest two models
    vs 6.0% for the rest (p < 0.001).
  - GPT-5.1 was least reproducible (within-case SD 2.69) and sat in the HIGHER catastrophic tier
    despite a middle-tier mean accuracy.
  - Conclusion: "models with indistinguishable mean accuracy carried different catastrophic-failure
    tiers and reproducibility profiles."

Bound, stated plainly: single-centre proof-of-concept, 54 cases, six models, physician-scored
diagnostic-safety ratings on challenging emergency cases — not lay-language triage dispositions
under this protocol's gold standard. The effect sizes are not ours. What transfers is the
structural claim, which is about estimators rather than about models: if dangerous outputs are
predominantly stochastic, a central-tendency aggregation rule cannot see them.

