EDIT 1 — SPECIFICATION. Report an any-run critical-miss rate, and record what the
new evidence says about the still-open DECISION 3
Sections affected: §0 (DECISION 3 evidence note); §8.1 (primary analysis)

The gap in OUR protocol: DECISION 3 offers exactly two options for "the primary-analysis unit for
every model output" — majority-of-three and first-run-primary. **Both are central-tendency
estimators.** Neither is a tail estimator. Under majority-of-three, a dangerous output occurring on
one run of three is arithmetically invisible; under first-run-primary it enters with probability
one in three. A grep of the protocol for any-run, per-run, or worst-case reporting returns nothing
but the DECISION 3 row itself.

Why the existing instability metric does not discharge this. §5.6 already reports run-to-run
agreement "so that nondeterminism is measured rather than averaged away," and §4.2 carries
run-to-run instability as a secondary endpoint. The intent is right and the statistic is
insufficient, for a reason this protocol has already accepted elsewhere: **instability is
symmetric.** A model alternating between two safe dispositions and a model alternating between a
safe disposition and a critical miss have the same instability and very different safety profiles.
This is precisely the objection §7.3 sustains against symmetric agreement coefficients — which is
why Gate 3 is stated as a conditional, directional rate. The same objection applies to run
aggregation and has not yet been applied there.

Scope discipline — what this edit costs. **Nothing.** The repeated runs are already performed
(§5.6, at least three per model per condition) and the per-run results are already retained (§7.3.1
makes `run_margin` a data-capture requirement settled before unblinding). The any-run rate is a
second statistic computed from data the study already collects. No additional model calls, no
scenarios, no participants, and **no bearing on the §8.4 power calculation** that is blocking
filing. It is also explicitly not a second primary endpoint: no hypothesis test is nominated on it
and it joins no multiplicity family.

What this edit does NOT do: it does not decide DECISION 3. That is the PI's. It records the
evidence bearing on the choice in the same form §0 already uses for DECISION 1, and it makes the
safety statistic robust to whichever way the PI resolves it.

