EDIT 2 — CITATION. Add the ACEP AI Task Force consensus to §11
Section affected: §11 (reporting and regulatory context)

§11 currently cites EU AI Act Art. 72, FDA PCCP guidance, IMDRF N81/N88, NIST AI RMF, and WHO LMM
guidance, then closes with "What none of these supplies is the measurement." Every framework listed
is a regulator or a standards body. **There is no professional-society position anywhere in the
protocol** — ACEP appears nowhere in the document — and for a protocol about AI in the emergency
care pathway, the US emergency-medicine specialty body is the most directly relevant professional
voice available.

The consensus says this protocol's premise in its own words, which is worth more than a
paraphrase: AI integration across ED workflows, triage among them, "has outpaced clinician
training, vendor evaluation infrastructure, and specialty-wide governance"; most emergency
physicians "lack the foundational skills to critically evaluate or safely oversee these products";
and "no national standard exists to guide education, vetting, or implementation." Its second
priority — a clinician-led, expert-derived framework for vetting AI tools before deployment — is
the activity this protocol instruments.

This is an official work product of the ACEP AI Task Force, formally endorsed by the ACEP Board of
Directors in 2025, with Stanford, Yale and George Washington emergency-medicine authorship. It
strengthens §11's existing argument rather than changing it, and it is the kind of citation an IRB
and a reviewer weigh.

