NOT PROPOSED — recorded so the reasoning is auditable

1. Lange et al., jAImes Parkinson's chatbot real-world evaluation (Lancet Reg Health Eur
   2026;70:101866, doi 10.1016/j.lanepe.2026.101866, PMID 42761851). LIBRARY ONLY, but the single
   most interesting item this week after Liu, and the PI should know why it is not an edit.
   2035 conversations over 129 days of a deployed patient-facing RAG system, with the CARE-LLM
   conversation-level post-market-surveillance framework. Automated triage rated 88.6% "good"; expert
   review of 45 flagged conversations confirmed five critical events; and independent re-review of
   100 randomly sampled "good"-rated conversations found four more critical errors the automated
   triage had MISSED (4/100, 95% CI 1.6-9.8) — a conditional false-negative rate within the
   "good" stratum, which the authors are careful to say is not an overall critical-event rate.
   Confirmed failures included explicit suicidal ideation that did not trigger the intended
   emergency response.
   Why no edit: it corroborates two positions the protocol already holds — that automated quality
   metrics miss clinically critical failures and that expert adjudication is required to find them
   (§7.4 physician sovereignty), and that encounter-time measurement needs a post-market successor
   (§10's S2/S3 boundary). Corroboration of a standing position is not a reason to edit. It is,
   however, a strong candidate citation if the PI ever writes the successor-study section, and its
   crisis-safeguard failure is directly relevant to the protocol's crisis-safeguard consistency
   sub-study.

2. Marecka et al., two generations of AI triage tools (JMIR AI 2026;5:e84469, doi 10.2196/84469,
   PMID 42772750). LIBRARY ONLY. 116,890 real virtual-triage encounters; structured questionnaire
   (86%) vs LLM conversational interface (14%) sharing the SAME validated Bayesian engine, which
   isolates interaction mode. Conversational elicited more findings (median 36 vs 32, P<.001) and
   higher self-reported intended adherence, most at the acuity extremes (emergency 23.7% vs 10.5%).
   Why no edit, and this one needs care: it is tempting to read it as support for the register
   factor, and it is not. Users SELF-SELECTED their interface, so demographic and motivational
   confounding is uncontrolled by design, and the outcome is self-reported INTENT rather than
   behaviour or disposition accuracy. The authors present it as observational. Our register factor
   is randomised within scenario and scored against a physician gold standard. Citing this for the
   register rationale would import an observational, self-selected, intent-based result into a
   randomised, gold-standard-scored claim.

3. Waisberg & Guarnieri, "Agentic AI in Medicine: ... the Case for Clinical Testing Harnesses"
   (Ann Biomed Eng 2026, doi 10.1007/s10439-026-04381-6, PMID 42776404). LIBRARY CANDIDATE ONLY —
   a Letter, conceptual, no data. Noted because its proposed testing harness (scenario libraries
   from clinical edge cases, full-trajectory observability, explicit escalation testing, staged
   evidence thresholds) describes something close to this protocol's instrument, for agentic
   systems. Relevant if the protocol is ever extended to tool-using agents; nothing to cite today.

4. The npj Digital Medicine stigmatizing-information triage study (doi 10.1038/s41746-026-03270-5,
   added by the reconciler 21 Sep, source "scite-verified") is **NOT INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED** — it
